> Tom Sullivan <tsullivan@fwi.com> writes:
> 
> > The last Linux system I ran was Slackware 7 on an Intel based
> > system. It worked a little differently than this system. When the
> > Debian system boots, it goes right to X-windows. I don't want it to
> > do that, I just want it to go to a regular text terminal. I am going
> > to run this as a server, and see no reason to have X-windows
> > running, unless I am doing some other work on the system. I just
> > want to be able to run X-windows by typing "startx" when I want to
> > run it. What do I need to change to get it to not load X-windows
> > when the system boots?
> 
> Look in /etc/inittab, find a line that looks something like
> 
> id:5:initdefault:
> 
> and change the 5 to a 3.

Debian doesn't distinguish multi-user with and without X, so initdefault
is already 2. Instead edit /etc/X11/default-display-manager to read
/usr/bin/false instead of /usr/bin/xdm and the box will go to multi-user
without X. When it's already up with X, issue "/etc/init.d/xdm stop" as
root on the console (for getting to console see below).

> > Also, when in X-windows, how do I quit it and go back to a text
> > terminal? As I recall from my old Slackware system, there was a
> > control key combination you could press to do this, but that doesn't
> > seem to work with this system.
> 
> When you are using the graphical login, there isn't usually a way to
> quit from X completely.  If you started X with "startx", there is most
> likely some "exit", or "logout" button or menu entry you can use to
> quit X.

Some display manager have a button for termination (after entering the
root password). But pressing Ctrl-Alt-F1 should give you the console
(F1--F12 all lead to different virtual consoles of which F7 should give
you the X11 session, F8 and up are usually unused while F1-F6 give text
logins). From one of the text virtual consoles one can easily log in as
root and stop the X server as outlined above.

