brendon.oliver@gmail.com writes:

> Hi all,
>
> I have a Promise PCI IDE card in a PWS500au with 3x drives attached.
> Beein installed for over a year - no problems at all (running a Debian
> 3.0 + testing hybrid).   Until the weekend, the card has always been in
> the topmost PCI slot (looking at the back of the case).  I needed to
> shift the card to make room for a new PCI card so relocated it to one
> of the lower slots (2nd from the bottom I think it was).  However,

Don't do that.  The PWS has a chipset bug that causes data corruption
on DMA transfers crossing a page boundary to the 64-bit slots.

> after doing so the machine refused to boot - no screen display at all
> (just those clicking noises it makes prior to flashing the LEDs on the
> keyboard during power up).  So I put the card back where it was -

To avoid problems from the DMA bug, SRM refuses to boot if it finds a
card in the 64-bit slots that isn't on its builtin list of cards known
not to be affected.  It's still supposed to display a message to this
effect.  The check can be disabled by setting some parameter in SRM.

> machine booted up fine.  But after it came back up I discovered that
> all 3 disks attached to that card appear to have been toasted!
>
> The drives are recongised as hde/hdg/hdh, but dmesg shows 'drive ready,
> seek, complete' type errors and the manufacturers / model details show
> as gibberish (e.g. one drive is listed as 'Fubitsu'); also all three
> are reported as the same size: 7398Mb (I think one was 8Gb, one was
> 30Gb, can't remember what the capacity  of the 3rd drive was).  I've
> tried running parted & fdisk on two of the drives which I don't care
> about with the following output:
>
> Device contains neither a valid DOS partition table, nor Sun, SGI or
> OSF disklabel.

[...]

> If I repeat the exercise - I get the same results: no partition table.
>
> Have I somehow managed to completely toast theses disks or what?
> Anyone care to venture a suggestion as to what might have happened?  If
> it's something I've unwittingly done by shifting that IDE card I'd
> really like to know so I don't make the same mistake twice! (not that
> I'm likely to now - it will remain in that spot for eternity now <g>)

It seems unlikely that you've toasted all the disks at once.  I'd
suggest you check the cables.  If that doesn't help, maybe you've
fried the controller.  Try the disks in another machine.

