> design etc. I've put a bigger hard disk, now i just need to put more RAM 
> into it, 128MB is a little too low.

A word of caution.  Be careful with what you put in the 64-bit PCI
slots.  Most importantly, don't put disk controllers there, or you'll
get trouble.  There is a bug in the Miata chipset (21174 aka Pyxis),
that corrupts data on DMA transfers to those slots.  Use them either
for cards that do not use DMA (or use it in a safe way), or things
where some corruption won't hurt, such as a graphics card.

> As a matter of interest, do you guys use these things for any real work, 
> or is it just a toy? I've installed openVMS on it and it runs fine, but 
> now i've got redhat linux and am trying to get mySQL running on it, but 
> it seems like it needs more than 128mb of ram to run properly.

MySQL doesn't use very much memory with a small database and few
connections.  A large DB and many connections will increase the memory
requirement.  Of course, 128MB is a little low, whatever you're doing.

As for what we use them for, I have an SX164 doing a fine job at a
variety of tasks, such as web server, mail server, a few mailing
lists, hosting some BitKeeper repos.  All in all, it's a fine machine,
rock solid hardware, not like the new PC gear.

